  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 312 PM EDT Sun Aug 30 2026\n\nTuesday through Thursday Night...\n\nThe forecast thinking through this time frame is unchanged from 24 \nhours ago.  Latest guidance continues to show a large 594 ridge \nbuilding across the Ohio Valley.  This will result in mostly sunny \ndays and mostly clear nights with temperatures gradually increasing \nthrough the period.  We'll likely see a gradient of high \ntemperatures across the region with the hottest air being confined \nto areas west of I-65 and slightly lower readings out in the \nBluegrass and I-75 corridors.  Dewpoints are still expected to \nremain in the 60s through the period.  We'll likely see good \natmospheric mixing each day which will result in some dry air \nmixdown in the afternoons.  This will keep our heat indices each \nafternoon in the upper 90s to the very low 100s east of I-65, with \nmainly 100-103 in areas west of I-65.  LREF heat index guidance \nhasn't changed that much with the probability of exceeding 100F on \nTuesday at 20-30% west of I-65.  By Wednesday, probabilities \nincrease to 40-60% west of I-65 and further increase to 50-70% by \nThursday. In the Bluegrass, the highest probs of 40-50% of exceeding \n100F on the heat index would be on Thursday. Not much relief will be \nfound at night as overnight minimums will be in the low-mid 70s with \nthe dense urban corridors likely only cooling to around 80.  With \nthe expected duration of heat over the coming week, Heat Advisories \nwill likely be issued for this time range in the next 24 hours or so.\n\nFriday through Sunday...\n\nMoving into the late week and into the weekend, the upper air \npattern will start to change slightly.  A large upper trough/closed \nlow is forecast to plow into the Pacific northwest while the mid-\nlevel ridge over the OV/SE US starts to flatten out and retrograde \nslightly to the west.  If this were to occur, our flow will become \nnorthwesterly and we'll have to watch for perturbations riding \naround the ridge which may bring MCSs back into the forecast.  A \nfair amount of spread remains in the long range ensembles here \nindicating that not all the members are suggesting the ridge \nretrograding.  The OP Euro trends have been stronger with the ridge \nretrograding and bringing some relief to the region.  For now, 20-\n30% POPs out in the Fri-Sun period look reasonable at this time. The \ntropics will become more active in this time period and may end up \ninfluencing the pattern than other synoptic scale features.  As such, \nthe forecast confidence here is lower than average.  For now, we'll \nlikely see low-mid 90s on Friday for highs, but things may cool back \ninto the upper 80s/lower 90s for Sat/Sun, especially if we get \nconvection back into the forecast.",
